<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Dutch translation.

    A translation of `values/strings.xml`, which is the source (SPEC §9). One
    Dutch for the whole catalogue: it serves Amsterdam and Antwerp alike, so
    nothing here belongs to the Netherlands alone or to Flanders alone. Where
    Android's own Dutch has a word, that word is used.

    Rules, the same as everywhere: positional placeholders, <plurals> for
    anything that agrees, and the `name` attributes left untouched. Dutch
    distinguishes two plural categories, `one` and `other`.

    The typography is Dutch: quotation marks are “ ”, the apostrophe is ’ and
    not the straight quote, which is why nothing here is escaped, and the dash
    that breaks a sentence is – with a space on either side rather than the em
    dash the English file uses. Compounds are written as one word, which is
    what lengthens the labels; where a button could not carry the literal
    translation it takes the shorter wording Android itself uses. The reader is
    addressed as `je`, as Android's Dutch does. See docs/glossary/nl.md.
-->
<resources xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">

